WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.130
(energetic tune riffing)

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:02.963
- [Cassandra Freeman] People, people,...

00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:04.680
- I love everything happening.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.720
- You know what, I'm just
so ready for spring to come.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.323
So, I was like, "Listen, make
me look like a sunflower.

00:00:09.323 --> 00:00:10.156
Okay?"

00:00:10.156 --> 00:00:10.989
(distant audience chuckling)

00:00:10.989 --> 00:00:12.540
- [Kelly] Um, and so can
you tell us a little about

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.830
what's going on this season in Bel Air?

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:14.827
What's going on with your character?

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:15.900
- [Cassandra Freeman] Oh, okay.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
So, Aunt Viv.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:20.310
Listen, if you are a woman
trying to figure out how to

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.590
get your life to the next
level, really anyone,...

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.240
I feel like Aunt Viv is that story.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.670
You know, Aunt Viv is so
great at giving you advice,

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:27.930
giving everyone else advice.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.150
And this season the mirror
has to be turned around.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.250
It's like, but how do you
move yourself forward?

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.400
So, the last episode, she
got kind of in trouble for

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.930
sort of standing up for
herself, saying like,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.480
the Neiman Fellowship, this
artistic thing that she got,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.920
is not what she thought.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.050
Like, she doesn't actually
get to be creatively free.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:43.883
It's so funny.

00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:45.330
All she wants to do is be creatively free,

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.250
and nobody will let her be great.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.380
Um, so this season you'll see like,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.590
what does it take for you
to claim and grab the torch

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:53.550
of your own greatness?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.380
And that means you have to
confront your own truth.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.000
And that truth might hurt other people,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.650
but it might set you free.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.750
- [Kelly] But being honest
in the end is better for

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:01.860
the person, even if they get hurt.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.100
I love a show that you love
and it's fun, and it's,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.270
but you learn something.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:07.290
I love that.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.240
So wait, your father, I
actually found this interesting.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.370
Your father, he was the
first black news anchor

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.720
in Jacksonville, Florida, right?

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:13.553
- That's right.

00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:14.386
- [Kelly] How does that come about?

00:01:14.386 --> 00:01:15.525
That's so...

00:01:15.525 --> 00:01:17.070
I always look up to those people.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.360
The fact that they can deliver...

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.070
Cause I've had to do really hard things,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.450
like hosting things sometimes,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.680
and talk about serious things.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.890
And I, I cannot keep
that emotion in, like,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:27.090
it just pours out of me.

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:27.923
I'm such an empath.

00:01:27.923 --> 00:01:28.756
- Yes, of course.

00:01:28.756 --> 00:01:30.510
- [Kelly] It's a talent to
be able to be on the news,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.250
and have to deliver things that are...

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.660
I just have such a respect.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.910
- Well, my dad, he kind of
fell into it accidentally.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.100
My dad, very magnanimous, like 6'7,

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.930
his name's Mack Freeman.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.840
He passed away years ago now,
like almost 20 years ago.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.010
But my father actually, he,
it was sort of like a setup

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.360
with a civil rights movement.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.550
And they were trying
to prove the point that

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.070
all of the, you know, stations
were basically racist,

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.990
'cause there were no black
people doing the news.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:56.370
So they were like, "Mack, you go in there,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.420
you try to sign up for a job."

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.253
So that's what he did.

00:01:58.253 --> 00:01:59.610
He goes in, he tries to get a job.

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.650
My dad has two degrees, a master's degree,

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:02.700
political science.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:03.533
Like he's a genius.

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:04.740
My, my father's a really smart man.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.000
Who wouldn't say that about their dad?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.130
And my dad, he goes in to
fill out the paperwork,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.230
and they're like, "Boy,
we're not giving away

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.340
janitor positions."

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.590
And so he was like, "Got you."

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.480
So, my father starts to walk out,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.120
and he says, "Hold up, maybe
we do have a job for him."

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.250
And so they made him an anchorman,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.860
but it was really because there
was so much social unrest.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.840
And they needed someone black to go into

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:25.673
the black neighborhoods.

00:02:25.673 --> 00:02:27.690
So my father became very famous.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:29.070
He was known as like Batman and Robin.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.140
Back then you'd actually
have your own cameraman.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.700
And so it was like him, and his friend,

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.260
and he became very famous as that.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.750
But then I grew up being
this little girl that would

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.190
always introduce my dad.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.677
And so it'd be like, I'd
be five and six years old,

00:02:39.677 --> 00:02:43.380
and be like, "Hello, my
father is Mack Freeman,

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.330
and he's the first black
television news reporter,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.200
second in the state.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.810
And you might know him,
because he did this and that."

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.450
And so I started at such a
young age that it was never

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.250
a big deal to talk in front of people.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.144
- I love that.

00:02:54.144 --> 00:02:54.977
- [Cassandra Freeman]
Because my dad would be like,

00:02:54.977 --> 00:02:56.490
"Yeah, when you're out
there there's nothing to be

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:57.323
nervous about.

00:02:57.323 --> 00:02:58.770
They're hoping that you
give them something.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.930
Inspiration, information, something."

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:01.763
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:01.763 --> 00:03:02.596
- Yeah.

00:03:02.596 --> 00:03:03.540
- That's a that's a pretty cool thing.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.470
Like a legacy.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.970
That, that's a, that's amazing.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:06.803
- Thank you.

00:03:06.803 --> 00:03:07.740
- [Kelly] Well you're, you're carrying on

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.840
your father's legacy by
focusing on diversity

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.250
in entertainment as well, right?

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:12.083
- Yeah.

00:03:12.083 --> 00:03:12.916
- I think that's so important.

00:03:12.916 --> 00:03:14.310
- Yeah, you know, I wish there was a...

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:16.050
We need to create a
new word for diversity.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.390
You know, it should be like
a word that means, like,

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.220
I want all my cool white friends to be

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.690
at the same table with me.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.523
You know?

00:03:22.523 --> 00:03:23.411
- Yeah.

00:03:23.411 --> 00:03:24.244
- Like if you're sitting
around as an artist

00:03:24.244 --> 00:03:25.770
in entertainment, sometimes
you might find yourself

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:26.603
in Oklahoma.

00:03:26.603 --> 00:03:27.510
You're like, "Who's gonna
do my hair, and makeup?"

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:29.077
Or you're trying to shoot
a film in Tennessee,

00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:30.360
"Who's gonna be the cameraman?"

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.193
Right?

00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:32.670
And what happens to so
many of us is, what,

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:34.200
you just call up the people you know.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.330
But what happens when even
the people you know are busy?

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:39.128
I like to think you come to
my company, Creatricity...

00:03:39.128 --> 00:03:39.961
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:39.961 --> 00:03:42.480
- And Creatricity is like
LinkedIn having a baby with IMDB.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.000
And IMDB is like the place
you go to find like where your

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.693
favorite actor and director is.

00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:47.526
- Yeah.

00:03:47.526 --> 00:03:49.230
- And so I hope that's what
Creatricity is for people.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.230
It's just a force, an
easier way for integration.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.114
I like to think...

00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:53.947
- [Kelly] Like networking, right?

00:03:53.947 --> 00:03:56.130
- Honey, it's networking, and
it's fun partying as well.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:56.963
- Yeah.

00:03:56.963 --> 00:03:57.796
(Kelly chuckling)

00:03:57.796 --> 00:03:58.629
- You know, so you come,
and just think if you're

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:01.260
in a room with vetted media
entertainment people,...

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.139
- Yeah.

00:04:02.139 --> 00:04:03.360
and so you don't have to be
like, "Why you here, Sugar?"

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.340
Instead it's like, "Oh, you
here, you must be somebody.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:06.930
So let me tell you what
I'm up to right now."

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.840
You know, it should be easy to
make your idea come to life.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.030
- [Kelly] Absolutely, without
getting so much friction

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:12.963
all the time,...

00:04:12.963 --> 00:04:13.796
- [Cassandra Freeman] Yeah.

00:04:13.796 --> 00:04:14.629
- or hurdle after hurdle.

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:15.462
- [Cassandra Freeman] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:15.462 --> 00:04:16.295
And also, like, we get tired of, like,

00:04:16.295 --> 00:04:18.060
collaborating =with the same
people over and over again.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:18.893
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:18.893 --> 00:04:19.778
- Sometimes you need new energy.

00:04:19.778 --> 00:04:20.611
- Well, you're just gonna
get the same product.

00:04:20.611 --> 00:04:21.450
You might, you wanna
do something different.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:22.283
Yeah, yeah.

00:04:22.283 --> 00:04:23.116
- [Cassandra Freeman] Exactly.

00:04:23.116 --> 00:04:24.570
Maybe something that no
one's even thought of before.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:25.403
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:04:25.403 --> 00:04:26.236
So, it's called Creatricity?

00:04:26.236 --> 00:04:27.090
- Yeah, like the electricity.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:27.923
- What a great name, yeah.

00:04:27.923 --> 00:04:28.756
- Thank you!

00:04:28.756 --> 00:04:29.618
- With creativity.

00:04:29.618 --> 00:04:30.451
I like it.

00:04:30.451 --> 00:04:31.284
- Thank you.

00:04:31.284 --> 00:04:32.520
(distant audience applauding)

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.800
- [Kelly] All right, season
two of Bel Air is streaming

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.323
on Peacock with new
episodes every Thursday.

